<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sigismund I the Old</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1467">1467</a>–<a href="/years/1548">1548</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sigismund I of Poland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sigismund I the Old was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and grand duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1506 to 1548. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-krakow">Treaty of Kraków</a> in 1525 he accepted the homage of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>, who secularised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s lands as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> under Polish overlordship. His reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> flourish at the royal court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old.jpg" alt="Sigismund I the Old" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans von Kulmbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kulmbach_Sigismund_I_the_Old.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
